        """
    summary """
        The screen ran and decided (honest negative, per the Phase 5 convention that a completed screen is a passing observation whatever direction it reports). Across 14 exactly-matched-rate cells (7 test-set photographs at 1 and 2 bpp, measured encoded to neutral's achieved byte count, both decoded with the pinned djxl) the measured per-transform scale improved butteraugli in 5 cells and regressed 9, mean +2.03% and median +1.18% with a range of -5.9% to +14.2%, and regressed SSIMULACRA2 in 11 of 14 (mean -0.41). Measured lands 0.55% under the cap on average, worth about +0.55% butteraugli on this corpus's local RD slope, so roughly a quarter of the mean is that confound and the rest is real. Production remains CoverSizePenalty::Neutral and the correction is not carried into Phase 6.3.
        """
    supersedes [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-2b-quality-negative-2026-08-12/1 ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-3-csf-agreement-fail-2026-08-12/1 : evidence {
    title "Phase 6.3 pre-registered agreement check: the CSF fails both criteria, in a diagnosed direction"
    state verified
    result pass
    method observation
    observed_at git:7e2b03759e368199cd0856aa824ec80e10170a99
    artifact ".agent/scratch/phase6-3-csf-weight-2026-08-12/csf-vs-measured.txt"
    summary """
        The check ran against its pre-registered tolerance and the verdict is FAIL: worst bin ratio 3.14x against a 1.35x limit and Pearson r +0.408 against a 0.80 limit, with the tolerance, the 60 ppd viewing assumption and the 2p = 0.8924 conversion all fixed before any number was computed and no parameter adjusted afterwards. Two distinct disagreements: Mannos-Sakrison attenuates towards DC (bin 0 predicted 0.830) where the measurement makes the lowest non-LLF frequencies the most sensitive (1.915), the known detection-versus-distortion-visibility divergence; and it rolls off far too hard at high frequency (top bin predicted 0.335 against 1.050 measured, where the measured curve rises and the model still falls)
        """
}

