record jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-0-quality-screen-2026-08-12/1 : evidence {
    title "Phase 7.0 screen: largest quality movement on the track, but butteraugli and SSIMULACRA2 split sharply"
    state verified
    result pass
    method observation
    observed_at git:ecf5cc1303e2b26ab0baf10480e903522ffef9aa
    artifact "JPXL/docs/experiments/2026-08-12-rate-distortion-quantizer.md"
    summary """
        28 cells on the Phase 6.2b/6.3/6.5 corpus and protocol. Pooled butteraugli better in 20 of 28 (mean -5.21%, best cell -29.9%) and SSIMULACRA2 better in only 4 of 28 (mean -1.80). By rate: 0.5 bpp butteraugli 5/7 (mean -2.08%) with SSIMULACRA2 4/7 (mean -0.72); 1 bpp butteraugli 5/7 (mean -8.79%) with SSIMULACRA2 0/7 (mean -3.00); 2 bpp butteraugli 4/7 (mean -4.29%) with SSIMULACRA2 0/7 (mean -2.36); 4 bpp butteraugli 6/7 (mean -5.66%) with SSIMULACRA2 0/7 (mean -1.10). At 0.5 and 1 bpp byte deltas are -0.34% and -0.43% so those rows are properly matched and the metric split is not a rate artifact. At 2 and 4 bpp the RD arm lands 3.0% and 5.7% under the cap (worst -7.8%) because a smaller stream at every quantizer setting worsens the known Phase 4M high-rate undershoot, so those two rows are confounded and flagged as such. Contract B fails on material SSIMULACRA2 loss; production remains QuantizerChoiceMode::Nearest
        """
}

record jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-0-rd-implementation-2026-08-12/1 : evidence {
    title "Phase 7.0 RD quantizer implemented; nearest still bit-identical, RD provably minimises its objective"
    state verified
    result pass
    method command
    observed_at git:ecf5cc1303e2b26ab0baf10480e903522ffef9aa
    command "cargo test --workspace"
    summary """
        Nearest is the default and an explicitly-nearest request encodes byte-identically to it, while the rate-distortion arm provably reaches the wire. rate_distortion_choice_minimises_its_own_objective_and_widens_the_dead_zone checks over every square transform and 24 target magnitudes per cell that no candidate beats the chosen one on residual_bits + rd*error^2 recomputed independently from the public surface, and that the RD rule never selects a larger magnitude than nearest -- a rate term can only make coefficients cheaper, so the dead zone can only widen -- plus that the mode is not inert. Full workspace suite green and cargo fmt --all --check clean
        """
}

record jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-1-quality-screen-2026-08-12/1 : evidence {
    title "Phase 7.1 screen: selectivity halves the SSIMULACRA2 damage but does not remove it"
    state verified
    result pass
    method observation
    observed_at git:236dd69b4cf58d4d35ad2a01ffb4c46538c8d6b2
    artifact "JPXL/docs/experiments/2026-08-12-trailing-truncation.md"
    summary """
        28 cells on the Phase 7.0 corpus, rates and protocol. Pooled SSIMULACRA2 better in 3 of 28 (mean -0.68) and butteraugli better in 19 of 28 (mean -3.46%). Against Phase 7.0's per-coefficient rate term on the identical protocol (SSIMULACRA2 4/28, mean -1.80; butteraugli 20/28, mean -5.21%) selectivity cut the SSIMULACRA2 damage by 62% while keeping most of the butteraugli gain, confirming the design hypothesis. The gate still fails under the SSIMULACRA2-primary decision, so production stays QuantizerChoiceMode::Nearest. Byte deltas by rate are -1.38/-0.36/-2.00/-3.98%, so the 1 bpp row is clean and the high-rate rows partly confound the known undershoot; s7/0.5 is an outlier whose flat baseline SSIMULACRA2 is already negative and which dominates the 0.5 bpp mean (excluding it that mean is -0.25 rather than -0.95)
        """
}

record jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-1-truncation-proof-2026-08-12/1 : evidence {
    title "The truncation pass provably shortens the walk rather than zeroing by magnitude"
    state verified
    result pass
    method command
    observed_at git:236dd69b4cf58d4d35ad2a01ffb4c46538c8d6b2
    command "cargo test -p jpxl-encode-policy --release --test truncation_saving -- --nocapture"
    summary """
        Checked against the real jpxl_encode::vardct::walk_frame rather than a restatement of the same assumption. On a 256x256 mixed-content fixture at 1 bpp the pass removes 11.8% of nonzero tokens (5,513 to 4,863) and collapses 42.6% of zero tokens (13,907 to 7,988), total symbols 20,098 to 13,529. That asymmetry is the signature being tested: a magnitude rule would move the two counts together and a mid-run rule would raise the zero count. non_zeros symbol count is unchanged at 678, confirming the pass touches coefficients and never the cover. A second test pins that the default target-rate policy is byte-identical with the pass merely available
        """
}

record jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-1a-gap-measured-2026-08-12/1 : evidence {
    title "residual_bits prices only 21.7-50.9% of the symbols the encoder emits"
    state verified
    result pass
    method command
    observed_at git:31649d85636095103b2f97be8400784ea3b346ab
    command "JPXL_RATE_REF=<ref.ppm> JPXL_RATE_BPP=<0.5|1|2> cargo test -p jpxl-encode-policy --release --test rate_proxy_gap -- --ignored --nocapture"
    artifact ".agent/scratch/phase7-1a-rate-proxy-gap-2026-08-12/measurements.txt"
    summary """
        Driving jpxl_encode::vardct::walk_frame (the writer's own walk, not a reimplementation) over test-set/20240501_110934 at the promoted target-rate policy: residual_bits prices 21.7% of emitted symbols at 0.5 bpp, 33.7% at 1 bpp and 50.9% at 2 bpp, so it is blind to 78.3%, 66.3% and 49.1% respectively. Zero-valued coefficient tokens -- real emitted symbols that it charges nothing for -- are 73.1%, 62.9% and 45.8% of all coefficient tokens. The truncation lever: zeros sitting immediately before each block's last nonzero number 0.91, 1.56 and 1.43 per varblock-channel, so zeroing one block's last nonzero frees its own token plus about 1.56 more at 1 bpp where residual_bits credits a flat ~2 bits regardless and credits the same for zeroing mid-run where nothing is freed
        """
}

record jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-1a-lever-identified-2026-08-12/1 : evidence {
    title "Phase 7.1a identifies walk truncation as the lever and forces a block-level design"
    state verified
    result pass
    method observation
    observed_at git:31649d85636095103b2f97be8400784ea3b346ab
    artifact "JPXL/docs/experiments/2026-08-12-rate-proxy-gap.md"
    summary """
        The lever is walk truncation, not magnitude. I.4 emits a coefficient token for every order position up to the last nonzero and then stops, so zeroing the last nonzero frees its own token plus every interior zero back to the previous nonzero, while zeroing mid-run frees nothing. residual_bits is indifferent between those two decisions, which is why Phase 7.0 zeroed by magnitude alone and stripped texture uniformly. Because the last-nonzero position is a property of the whole block, a per-coefficient rule is structurally incapable of this selectivity whatever its lambda -- so the design must be a block-level backward pass over trailing nonzeros, pricing each drop at its true saving. The record also states what the measurement does not establish: token counts are exact but token bits are not, since each symbol's real cost depends on an ANS cluster the census builds after the walk, and a within-block pass ignores the neighbour-prediction coupling between blocks
        """
}

record jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-2-default-byte-identical-2026-08-12/1 : evidence {
    title "Phase 7.2: unit lambda_scale is byte-identical; the control reaches the wire under trailing truncation"
    state verified
    result pass
    method command
    observed_at git:b949b17f4d04083487c87a6ac2ab56d7f020e0ed
    command "cargo test -p jpxl-encode-policy --lib lambda_scale_defaults_to_one_and_scales_the_derived_weight the_nearest_quantizer_is_the_default_and_byte_identical the_donor_weight_is_promoted_for_target_rate_only -- --nocapture"
    summary """
        lambda_scale defaults to 1.0 on EncodeRequest::defaults and, after promotion, 4.0 on for_target. An explicit lambda_scale of 1.0 is byte-identical to hierarchical_request under the fixed-quantizer path. Scale 2.0 doubles the derived lambda channel-for-channel. Under TrailingTruncation, scale 4.0 produces a different codestream from unit scale on a 256x256 fixture, proving the control reaches the wire. Fixed-quantizer defaults stay QuantizerChoiceMode::Nearest; for_target carries TrailingTruncation at lambda_scale 4.0.
        """
}

record jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-2-operating-point-decided-2026-08-12/1 : evidence {
    title "Phase 7.2 decides: lambda_scale 4.0 under trailing truncation is the operating point; promoted on for_target"
    state verified
    result pass
    method observation
    observed_at git:b949b17f4d04083487c87a6ac2ab56d7f020e0ed
    artifact "JPXL/docs/experiments/2026-08-12-lambda-calibration.md"
    summary """
        An operating point exists where SSIMULACRA2 is non-regressing and butteraugli retains its gain: trailing truncation at lambda_scale 4.0. Scale 2.0 also clears the mean gate but is weaker (SSIM 15/28). Scale 8.0 keeps the SSIM win but loses butteraugli on the 4 bpp row (+6.46% mean) and deepens undershoot. EncodeRequest::for_target now sets quantizer_choice=TrailingTruncation and lambda_scale=4.0; EncodeRequest::defaults stays nearest at unit lambda so the Contract A fixed-quantizer path is unchanged.
        """
}

